## Introduction

Each cell carries a local linear function of the active coordinates
centred at the cell's centre, g(x) = mu_k + beta_k'(x_A - c_k), in place
of a constant; the cell update draws the (d + 1)-vector from its
conjugate normal and the structural moves integrate it out. This is the
Voronoi analogue of model trees in a BART ensemble (Prado, Moral and
Parnell 2021, MOTR-BART). Reach for it when the truth is locally linear
and rows are few: constant cells need many small cells, linear cells
need few large ones. Mean ensemble only; needs min-max scaled columns.

Prerequisite: BART (Chipman, George and McCulloch 2010) and AddiVortes
(Stone and Gosling 2025).

## The model

The prior (mu_k, beta_k) ~ N(0, sigma_mu^2 I) and the integrated
likelihood in the (d + 1)-dimensional form, from `docs/models.md`,
"Linear cell basis". Link to `crates/thiessen/src/cells.rs`.

## Example

```{r example}
source("_setup.R")
data <- smooth_surface(n = 150, sd = 0.2, seed = 1, f = "linear")
control <- thiessen_control(tessellations = 20, general_params = schedule)
linear <- thiessen_control(
  tessellations = 20, general_params = schedule,
  mean_params = term_params(cell = cell_params(basis = "linear"))
)
fits <- paired_fits(data, list(default = control, linear = linear), seeds)
summary(fits$linear[[1]])
score(fits, data)
```

No external comparator: MOTR-BART has no CRAN package; it is cited as
the precedent.

## What it shows

Figure 1, mechanism: a transect at x2 = 0.5, truth, constant-cell fit,
linear-cell fit, steps against pieces.

```{r mechanism}
```

Figure 2, comparison: paired RMSE and width per seed beside the table
above; second panel, the distribution over draws of
`thiessen_diagnostics()$cell_count`, constant against linear: the linear
basis uses fewer cells.

```{r comparison}
```

Figure 3, boundary: the RMSE gap at n = 150 against n = 500; the gap
closes as the constant basis gets the rows it needs.

```{r boundary}
```

